/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-2","number":2,"title":"Session View","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"2.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-2-1-101","code":"2.1","title":"2.1","blurb":"2.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l2-1-1","title":"2.1.1 — Session anatomy diagram","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.1 — Session anatomy diagram","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can point to every Session View part the manual names and say what it is for."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open a Set and you land in Session View. The Note manual lists six parts in that view: tracks, clips, scenes, Set settings, a mixer, and a play/stop button. Those six pieces are the whole sketching surface. Note does not give you an Arrangement View timeline."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Set name sits at the top of the screen. The back arrow to the left of that name returns you to the Sets Library. The <strong>Set Settings</strong> icon sits to the right of the name and opens tempo, metronome, key and scale, and audio export. The mixer, the scene-number toggle, <strong>Undo</strong>/<strong>Redo</strong>, and the global <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong> button live in the bottom bar."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tracks run vertically. Scenes are horizontal rows of clips. Empty clip slots sit under the track headers as a grid of squares. A Set can hold at most eight tracks and eight scenes. A clip can be at most 16 bars long."},{"type":"prose","html":"Learn this map before you add clips. Every later Session action — launch, stop, mute, solo, export — lives in one of these six places."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note and tap <strong>+ New</strong> so a Set opens in Session View.","Point to the Set name, then to the back arrow on its left and the <strong>Set Settings</strong> icon on its right.","Point to the three default track headers and the empty clip-slot grid under them.","Point to the bottom bar. Find <strong>Undo</strong>/<strong>Redo</strong>, <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong>, <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong>, and <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong>.","Close the page. Recite the six Session parts from memory, then look back at the screen and confirm each one."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the six Session View parts listed in the manual. <strong>Answer:</strong> Tracks, clips, scenes, Set settings, a mixer, and a play/stop button.","Where do you return to the Sets Library from Session View? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name.","What are Note's Session maxima for tracks, scenes, and clip length? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View intro; §4.1 Tracks; §4.2 Clips; §4.3 Scenes; §4.4 Mixer; §4.5 Session View Actions","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Take a screenshot of Session View. Label six parts: tracks, clips, scenes, Set Settings, mixer, Play/Stop. Keep the screenshot with this Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-1-2","title":"2.1.2 — Set name + back to library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.2 — Set name + back to library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can leave Session View for the Sets Library and get back into the same Set without losing your place in the library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Set name sits at the top of Session View. It is the title of the file you are in, not a transport control. To the left of that name is a back arrow. The manual is explicit: you always get back to the Sets Library by tapping that arrow."},{"type":"prose","html":"That same back-arrow position does different work once you leave Session View for Instrument View. From Instrument View, the arrow to the left of the Set name returns you to Session View, not to the library. Learn the two hops as a stack: Instrument View → Session View → Sets Library. One tap never jumps both levels."},{"type":"prose","html":"The library is where you preview Sets, open another Set, or create a new one with <strong>+ New</strong>. Session View is where you build tracks, clips, and scenes. Mixing the two screens is how people think they \"lost\" a Set. You did not. You are one arrow away from the library list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note auto-saves about every 10–15 seconds, so leaving Session View with the back arrow does not require a separate Save command."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open any Set so you are looking at Session View and can read the Set name at the top.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name. Confirm you are in the Sets Library and that this Set is still listed.","Tap the Set again to reopen Session View. Confirm the same name is at the top.","Tap an empty <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon so Instrument View opens. Tap the back arrow once. Confirm you landed in Session View, not the library.","From Session View, tap the back arrow again and find the Set in the library from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the back arrow do when you are already in Session View? <strong>Answer:</strong> It returns you to the Sets Library.","What does that same arrow do from Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> It returns you to Session View.","Do you need a separate Save before you leave Session View? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note auto-saves about every 10–15 seconds."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View intro (back arrow to Sets Library)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (back arrow from Instrument View to Session View)","Note FAQ: auto-save every 10–15 seconds"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open a Set, go into Instrument View, then walk back: Instrument View → Session View → Sets Library → reopen the Set. Time the four taps until they are automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-1-3","title":"2.1.3 — Set Settings icon location","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.3 — Set Settings icon location","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open Set Settings from Session View and name every page that lives there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To the right of the Set name is the <strong>Set Settings</strong> icon. The same control is the upper-right corner of a Note Set. Tap it to open Set-specific settings. This is not App Settings. App Settings open from the three dots in the Sets Library and cover Cloud, Link, Help, and Live Lite. Set Settings belong to the Set you have open."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual lists four jobs behind that icon: choose the tempo, switch the metronome on or off, pick a key and scale for the Set, or export the Set as an audio file. The FAQ adds <strong>Groove Amount</strong> in the same menu (Note 1.0.15 and later). If your Help version is older than 1.0.15, Groove Amount is not in this list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Export Audio in that menu is greyed out when the Set has no clips. That is a later lesson. For now, learn the door: top right, next to the Set name."},{"type":"prose","html":"You will live in this menu whenever you need a click, a BPM, a scale, or a bounce. Open it from Session View, not from the library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set in Session View.","Point to the Set name, then to the icon immediately to its right. Tap <strong>Set Settings</strong>.","Read the list. Find <strong>Metronome</strong>, <strong>Tempo</strong>, <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, and <strong>Export Audio</strong>. If your version is 1.0.15 or later, also find <strong>Groove Amount</strong>.","Close Set Settings and return to Session View. Open it again from memory.","Leave the Set for the library. Open App Settings from the three dots. Confirm that menu is a different screen from Set Settings."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the Set Settings icon? <strong>Answer:</strong> To the right of the Set name, in the upper-right corner of the Set.","Name the four jobs the manual lists in Set Settings. <strong>Answer:</strong> Tempo, metronome on/off, key and scale, and export audio.","How is Set Settings different from App Settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings belong to the open Set. App Settings open from the library three-dots menu and cover Cloud, Link, Help, and Live Lite."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View intro; §5 Set Settings","Note FAQ p.4: tempo, Groove Amount, and Key and Scale via the top-right Settings menu","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Global Groove Amount"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Set Settings five times in a row without hunting. Each time, say out loud which item you would use for click, BPM, scale, and bounce."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-1-4","title":"2.1.4 — Vertical tracks vs horizontal scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.4 — Vertical tracks vs horizontal scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can read the Session grid as vertical tracks and horizontal scenes, and you can say which direction is which."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tracks are arranged vertically, similar to Live's Session View. Each column is one instrument path: a Drum Rack, a bass or other melodic preset, a Sampler, or — on Note 2.0.1 and later — an audio track you add with the <strong>Audio</strong> button. You can have at most eight tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scenes are the other axis. A scene is the set of clips that sit on the same horizontal row. Launch the scene number and every clip in that row starts together. A Set can have at most eight scenes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Read the grid as a table. Down a track you stack variations of one part. Across a scene you stack the parts that play at the same time. That is how you turn one loop into a song without an Arrangement View."},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty squares under a track header are empty clip slots, not extra tracks. Adding a clip does not add a track. Adding a track (swipe left, or the 2.x <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> buttons) adds a new vertical column."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new Set. Count the three default vertical tracks.","Look under the headers. Those empty squares are clip slots on those tracks, not new tracks.","Tap <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> in the bottom bar so the scene numbers appear at the left of each row.","Point down one track and say \"variations of one part.\" Point across one numbered row and say \"one section.\"","Recite the two maxima from memory: eight tracks, eight scenes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which way do tracks run? <strong>Answer:</strong> Vertically. Each column is one track.","What is a scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> The clips that sit on the same horizontal row.","What are the two Session maxima on those axes? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8 tracks and 8 scenes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (vertical, similar to Live Session View; max eight)","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (horizontal row; max eight)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks; up to eight scenes"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On paper, draw a 5-by-3 grid. Label columns as tracks (drums, bass, chords, lead, extra) and rows as scenes (full idea, drums+bass, add lead). You will build this skeleton in Module 2.2 and 2.4."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-1-5","title":"2.1.5 — Bottom bar: Undo/Redo, mixer, scene numbers, Play/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.5 — Bottom bar: Undo/Redo, mixer, scene numbers, Play/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can name every control in the Session View bottom bar and use each one without hunting."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The bottom of the Session View grid holds the Session View Actions. The manual lists four: <strong>Undo</strong>/<strong>Redo</strong>, the Set's mixer, a toggle to show or hide scene numbers, and a global <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong> button."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> reverse or restore the last change in the Set. Use them after a bad launch, a deleted clip, or a mixer move you did not mean."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> opens or closes the mixer strip. Mute (<strong>M</strong>), solo (<strong>S</strong>), and volume sliders live there. They are not on the grid until you show the mixer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> reveals the scene numbers at the left of each row. You tap a scene number to launch every clip in that row. Hide the numbers when you want more grid. Show them when you are arranging sections."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong> is global transport. It starts or stops the Set. Launching a clip also starts playback. Stopping a single clip uses the square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon on that clip, not this global button. Global Stop stops everything."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep one finger near this bar. You will use it every time you sketch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set in Session View and look only at the bottom bar.","Tap <strong>Undo</strong>, then <strong>Redo</strong>, after you move a track or open a slot, so you feel both arrows.","Tap <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong>. Confirm mute, solo, and volume sliders appear. Hide the mixer again.","Tap <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong>. Confirm numbers appear at the left of each row. Hide them, then show them again.","Tap global <strong>Play</strong>, listen for transport, then tap <strong>Stop</strong>. Repeat the four controls from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the four Session View Actions in the bottom bar. <strong>Answer:</strong> Undo/Redo, the mixer, show/hide scene numbers, and global Play/Stop.","Which control launches every clip in a row? <strong>Answer:</strong> A visible scene number. Show scene numbers first if they are hidden.","Does global <strong>Stop</strong> stop one clip or the whole Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> The whole Set. One clip uses the square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon on that clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.5 Session View Actions","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (Show/Hide Scene Numbers)","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (Show/Hide Mixer)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (Stop Clip icon on a playing clip)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With transport stopped, hide mixer and scene numbers. Then, without looking at this page, show mixer, show scene numbers, tap Play, tap Stop, and Undo once. Time yourself."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-1-6","title":"2.1.6 — Note Session View compared with Live Session View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.6 — Note Session View compared with Live Session View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.1 — Session map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can say what Note Session View shares with Live Session View and what Note does not give you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Note manual says tracks are arranged vertically, similar to Live's Session View. That is the sibling relationship. Both products use a clip grid: tracks as columns, scenes as rows, clips you launch. If you already know Live Session, the Note grid will feel familiar."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note is not Live on a phone. Note has no Arrangement View. You sketch, capture, sample, and send. Linear arrangement, full timeline editing, and desktop mixing happen in Live after you transfer the Set. Cloud and <code>.ablbundle</code> files move Note Sets into Live 11.2.5 or later. Live Sets cannot come back into Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note's grid is smaller by design. A Set holds at most eight tracks and eight scenes. A clip is at most 16 bars. Live Session on the desktop is not bound by those Note maxima."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this comparison so you stop looking for Arrangement, MIDI file import, AUv3, or pitch bend inside Note. Those are not here. Use the Session grid you have: launch clips and scenes, then send the Set when the idea is worth finishing in Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Note Set. Point down a track and across a scene. Say \"this is Session, not Arrangement.\"","Count the default tracks. Swipe toward <strong>Add Track</strong> and remember the cap is eight.","Show scene numbers and remember the cap is eight scenes.","Write one sentence: \"Note Session is a clip grid like Live Session. Note has no Arrangement View.\"","Recite three things Note Session does not do: Arrangement View, Live→Note Cloud, MIDI file import/export."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the Note manual say Note tracks share with Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tracks are arranged vertically, similar to Live's Session View.","Does Note include Arrangement View? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note is a Session grid only.","Can a Live Set transfer back into Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Sharing is Note → Live (and Note / Move / Push), not the other way."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (\"arranged vertically, similar to Live's Session View\")","Ableton Note Manual §1 Welcome (sketch, capture, sample; send Sets to Live; export audio)","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (Note Sets open in Live; Live Sets cannot open in Note)","Note FAQ: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips; no MIDI clip I/O; Live → Note not possible"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a card, draw two columns: \"Same as Live Session\" (vertical tracks, horizontal scenes, launch clips) and \"Note only / Note does not\" (8×8×16 limits, no Arrangement, no Live→Note). Keep the card in the Set notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-2-2-102","code":"2.2","title":"2.2","blurb":"2.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l2-2-1","title":"2.2.1 — Default three tracks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.1 — Default three tracks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open a new Set and identify the three default tracks and what they are preloaded with."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In a new Set you see three default tracks. That count comes from the Session View chapter. You do not start from an empty rack of columns. Note gives you three vertical tracks so you can play immediately."},{"type":"prose","html":"Each of those tracks is preloaded with a drum, bass, melodic instrument, or Sampler preset. The same manual also states that each track in a Set comes loaded with a random Track Preset. A Track Preset is an instrument plus two audio effects. Both statements are official. Expect three columns, each already holding some combination of drum / bass / melodic / Sampler, chosen as a random Track Preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1 and later, new Sets also remember the last key and tempo you used, default to C Major on a first new Set, and default new melodic tracks to a piano layout. Those 2.x defaults change how the tracks play, not the fact that you still get three of them."},{"type":"prose","html":"Three is the starting count, not the maximum. The maximum is eight. You add more in the next lesson."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From the Sets Library, tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","Count the vertical track headers. Confirm there are three.","Tap each track header to open Instrument View. Read the Track Preset name at the top. Note whether it is a drum, bass, melodic, or Sampler preset.","Return to Session View with the back arrow. Repeat on the other two tracks.","Recite from memory: a new Set starts with three tracks, each a random Track Preset from drum / bass / melodic / Sampler."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many tracks does a new Set show? <strong>Answer:</strong> Three.","What is loaded onto those tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> A drum, bass, melodic, or Sampler preset, as a random Track Preset (instrument plus two audio effects).","Is three the maximum? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The maximum is eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (three default tracks)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 Creating New Sets (drum, bass, melodic, or Sampler preset)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets (random Track Preset; instrument + two audio effects)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (last key/tempo, C Major, piano layout) — version-gated"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create two new Sets. Write the three preset names from each Set. Confirm both Sets start with three tracks even if the presets differ."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-2","title":"2.2.2 — Swipe left → Add Track (max 8)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.2 — Swipe left → Add Track (max 8)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can add tracks up to the official maximum of eight and stop when the grid is full."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A new Set starts with three tracks. You add more by swiping to the left and tapping the <strong>Add Track</strong> icon on the right. That is the pre-2.0 path in the Note manual. You can grow the Set until you have eight tracks total. The FAQ states the same cap: Note Sets contain a maximum of 8 tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1 and later, new add-track buttons appear: <strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, and <strong>Audio</strong>. Those buttons are a 2.x addition. They do not raise the cap. Eight is still eight. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> if you need to know which add-track UI you have."},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty clip slots under a track are not extra tracks. Swiping left to <strong>Add Track</strong> (or tapping <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> on 2.x) is the only documented way to add a column. Stay under the cap on purpose. A five-track sketch (drums, bass, harmony, lead, one extra) leaves three slots for later. Filling all eight on the first idea leaves no room to split a part."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new Set and count three tracks.","Swipe left. Tap <strong>Add Track</strong> (or, on 2.0.1+, pick <strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, or <strong>Audio</strong>). Count four.","Add tracks until you reach eight. Confirm you cannot add a ninth.","Long-press a spare track and use <strong>Delete</strong> until you are back at five. Confirm the grid still has room.","Recite the rule from memory: swipe left to add; maximum eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you add a track in the pre-2.0 manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe left and tap the <strong>Add Track</strong> icon on the right.","What is the maximum number of tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8.","Do the 2.x <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> buttons raise that maximum? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They change how you add a track. The cap stays 8."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (swipe left, Add Track, up to eight)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: add-track buttons Beat, Synth, and Audio"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build from three tracks to eight, then delete back to five. Write \"8 max\" on a note you keep with the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-3","title":"2.2.3 — Long-press reorder","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.3 — Long-press reorder","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can long-press a track and drag it to a new column without duplicating or deleting it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You long-press on tracks to drag them around and rearrange them. That is the whole reorder gesture. The same long-press also opens <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. Those are different commands. This lesson is only the drag."},{"type":"prose","html":"Reorder is visual, not musical by itself. The sound does not change because a drum track moved from column one to column three. What changes is how you read the grid. A useful studio habit — taken from beatmaking roles, not from a rename field — is to keep drums, then bass, then harmony, then lead from left to right. You do that by dragging, not by hoping the random Track Presets landed in that order."},{"type":"prose","html":"You cannot add a ninth track to make space. You can only shuffle the eight (or fewer) you have. If you want a new part in the middle, add a track first (if you are under eight), then long-press and drag it into place."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set with at least three tracks.","Long-press the rightmost track header until it lifts.","Drag it to the left so it becomes the first column. Release.","Long-press again and restore the original order.","Repeat the drag from memory, then tap <strong>Undo</strong> if you want the previous order back."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you reorder tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long-press a track and drag it to a new position.","Does the same long-press do anything else? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. It also offers <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. Reorder is the drag, not those commands.","Does moving a track change its sound? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only changes column order."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (long-press to drag and rearrange)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Track Commands (Duplicate and Delete on the same long-press)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: kick/bass/harmony/lead roles as a reading order"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drag the three default tracks into drums-left, bass-middle, melodic-right (or the closest match to the presets you got). Play the Set. Confirm the sound is the same and the grid is easier to read."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-4","title":"2.2.4 — Long-press Duplicate / Delete","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.4 — Long-press Duplicate / Delete","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can duplicate a track to copy its preset and clips, and you can delete a track you do not need."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press a track to access <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. Those commands live on the same press that lets you drag to reorder. Duplicate makes another track. Delete removes the one you pressed."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate is how you keep a drum kit and try a second kit, or keep a bass sound and start a lead on a copy. Each duplicate is still a track. It counts toward the eight-track maximum. If you already have eight tracks, you cannot duplicate until you delete one."},{"type":"prose","html":"Delete is permanent for that track and its clips. Use <strong>Undo</strong> in the bottom bar if you delete the wrong column. Note auto-saves every 10–15 seconds, so do not wait to notice a mistake."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not document a track-name field. After you duplicate, tell the two tracks apart by preset name, by Track Color (Note 1.7.2+), and by which clips they hold."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set with three tracks. Long-press the drum track (or the first track if you are not sure).","Tap <strong>Duplicate</strong>. Count four tracks. Confirm the new column carries the same Track Preset.","Long-press the duplicate. Tap <strong>Delete</strong>. Count three tracks again.","Duplicate once more so you have a spare. Confirm you are still under eight.","Recite from memory: long-press for <strong>Duplicate</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>; duplicates count toward eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where are Duplicate and Delete for a track? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the long-press menu on the track.","Does a duplicate count toward the eight-track maximum? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes.","What do you use if you delete the wrong track? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Undo</strong> in the Session View bottom bar."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (long-press Duplicate and Delete)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks; auto-save every 10–15 seconds","Note Release Notes 1.7.2: Track Color in the track menu"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate one track, then delete the copy. Repeat until the two commands feel distinct from the reorder drag on the same long-press."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-5","title":"2.2.5 — Track header opens Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.5 — Track header opens Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open Instrument View from a track header and return to Session View without adding a clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View is where you play pads, capture and edit notes, and shape the Track Preset. From Session View you can open it three ways: double-tap an existing clip, tap the track header, or tap the <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon in an empty slot (which also creates a new clip)."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is the middle path. Tap the track header. You enter Instrument View on that track without forcing a new clip into an empty slot. Use this when you want to hear the preset, change pads, or browse a different Track Preset before you commit a clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"The back arrow to the left of the Set name returns you to Session View. It does not return you to the library from here. One tap, one level."},{"type":"prose","html":"You will use the header constantly. Treat it as the door to the instrument, and treat <strong>Add Clip</strong> as the door that also creates a clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new Set so you have three tracks and an empty clip grid.","Tap the first track header. Confirm Instrument View opens and the empty slots did not gain a new clip.","Play a few pads. Do not capture yet.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name. Confirm you are in Session View and the grid is still empty.","Repeat on the other two headers from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the three documented ways to open Instrument View from Session View. <strong>Answer:</strong> Double-tap an existing clip, tap the track header, or tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> in an empty slot.","Which of those three also creates a new clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tapping <strong>Add Clip</strong> in an empty slot.","Where does the back arrow go from Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> Back to Session View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 Instrument View (header, double-tap clip, Add Clip)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (Add Clip opens Instrument View; back arrow returns to Session View)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open and close Instrument View from each of the three default headers. Then open it once via <strong>Add Clip</strong> and notice the extra clip in the grid. Delete that clip if you do not want it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-6","title":"2.2.6 — Label tracks by musical role","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.6 — Label tracks by musical role","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can assign each track a musical role so an eight-track Set stays readable."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official Note sources do not document a track-name field. You cannot \"label\" a track the way you rename a Set in Set Options. Teach roles anyway, because you only have eight tracks and you will lose the sketch if every column is just a random preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note names the parts that usually make a beat: kick (the pulse that works with the bass), 808/bass (the tuning reference for the root), snare (backbeat), hi-hat (divisions and style), a chord progression, and occasional shots. This course collapses those into a smaller role card you can keep on eight columns: drums, bass, harmony, lead, and one extra (shots / FX / second drums)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use what the app does give you. The Track Preset name at the top of Instrument View tells you drum vs melodic vs Sampler. On Note 1.7.2 and later, <strong>Track Color</strong> in the track menu marks a column. Long-press reorder so drums sit left, then bass, then harmony, then lead."},{"type":"prose","html":"Assign roles before you fill slots. A new Set's three random Track Presets may already cover drums, bass, and a melodic part. Keep them if they match the roles. Replace them if they do not."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new Set. Write five roles on paper: drums, bass, harmony, lead, extra.","Tap each of the three default headers. Write the Track Preset name next to the closest role.","If you are on 1.7.2 or later, long-press each track, set <strong>Track Color</strong>, and give each role a color you will reuse.","Reorder so the roles read left to right.","Recite the five roles from memory and point at the matching columns (or empty space you will add later)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you type a custom track name in Note, according to the sources? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources do not document a track-name field. Use preset name, color (1.7.2+), and column order.","Name the beatmaking parts you can map onto eight tracks. <strong>Answer:</strong> Kick, 808/bass, snare, hi-hat, chords, shots — or the shorter card drums / bass / harmony / lead / extra.","Why assign roles before you add more tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> The maximum is eight. Roles keep you from spending the last columns on duplicates of the same part."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note: kick, 808/bass, snare, hi-hat, chord progression, shots","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 and §7.1 (drum / bass / melodic / Sampler; Track Preset name)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 (reorder; max eight tracks)","Note Release Notes 1.7.2: Track Color in the track menu"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make a five-role card for this Set. Color or order the three default tracks to match. You will add the missing columns in Lesson 2.2.10."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-7","title":"2.2.7 — Track color","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.7 — Track color","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can change a track's color from the track menu on Note 1.7.2 or later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.7.2 added <strong>Track Color</strong>: change track color in the track menu. The track menu is the same long-press you already use for reorder, <strong>Duplicate</strong>, and <strong>Delete</strong>. Color is version-gated. If <strong>Settings → Help</strong> shows a version older than 1.7.2, this control is not there."},{"type":"prose","html":"Color does not change the sound. It marks the column. Because sources do not document a track-name field, color is the official visual label. Use a repeatable map: drums one color, bass another, harmony another, lead another. Keep that map across Sets so your eye learns it."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still identify the instrument by the Track Preset name in Instrument View. Color plus preset name plus column order is the whole labeling system the sources support."},{"type":"prose","html":"On versions before 1.7.2, skip this lesson's gesture. Use reorder and preset names only."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the Note version.","If the version is 1.7.2 or later, long-press a track header to open the track menu.","Choose <strong>Track Color</strong> and pick a color. Release and look at the header.","Color the other tracks with different colors. Confirm each column is distinct.","Recite from memory: long-press → track menu → <strong>Track Color</strong>. If your version is older, write that this feature is 1.7.2+."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In which version did Track Color arrive? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.7.2.","Where do you change it? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the track menu (long-press the track).","Does color change the sound? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only marks the track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.7.2: Track Color — change track color in track menu","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (long-press track commands)","Note FAQ: check version in Settings → Help"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Color a five-role skeleton (or the three default tracks plus two you add). Use the same color map you wrote in Lesson 2.2.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-8","title":"2.2.8 — 2.x add buttons: Beat, Synth, Audio","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.8 — 2.x add buttons: Beat, Synth, Audio","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can add a Beat, Synth, or Audio track on Note 2.0.1+ and still respect the eight-track cap."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1 added new add-track buttons: <strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, and <strong>Audio</strong>. That is a version-gated change to how you add a column. The pre-2.0 manual path is still true for older installs: swipe left and tap <strong>Add Track</strong>. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> before you look for the three buttons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, and <strong>Audio</strong> choose the kind of track you add. <strong>Audio</strong> is part of the 2.0.1 audio-track feature set (record loops, warped audio so tempo and pitch are independent on that path). Do not blend that warp rule into Sampler or Drum Rack. On Sampler and Drum Rack, the FAQ still applies: Transpose also changes playback speed, and warping is not available on that path."},{"type":"prose","html":"The cap does not change. You still have at most eight tracks. The buttons do not unlock a ninth column. On a pre-2.0 version you add generic tracks and load drum / bass / melodic / Sampler presets instead of picking <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> at add time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the version.","If you are on 2.0.1 or later, swipe or tap to add a track and find <strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, and <strong>Audio</strong>.","Add one <strong>Beat</strong> track and one <strong>Synth</strong> track. Count the total. Stay under eight.","If you add an <strong>Audio</strong> track, remember it is the 2.x warped-loop path, not a Sampler.","If you are below 2.0, swipe left and use <strong>Add Track</strong> instead. Write that the three buttons are 2.0.1+."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version added Beat, Synth, and Audio add-track buttons? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1.","What is the track maximum after those buttons arrived? <strong>Answer:</strong> Still 8.","Are 2.x audio-track loops warped the same way Sampler transpose works? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Audio tracks (2.0.1+) warp so tempo and pitch are independent. Sampler/Drum Rack transpose still changes speed (FAQ)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1: add-track buttons Beat, Synth, and Audio; audio loops are warped","Ableton Note Manual §4.1: swipe left, Add Track, max eight (pre-2.0 surface)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks; \"Warping is not currently available\"; Transpose also affects playback speed (Sampler/Drum Rack path)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, add one Beat, one Synth, and (optional) one Audio track to a Set that still has room. Delete any you do not need so you end at five tracks. On older versions, add two tracks with <strong>Add Track</strong> and write \"no Beat/Synth/Audio buttons on this version.\""},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-9","title":"2.2.9 — Default octaves for new bass vs melodic","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.9 — Default octaves for new bass vs melodic","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You know that Note 1.2 improved default octaves for new bass versus new melodic tracks, and you can check the octave of each new track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.2 lists this improvement: \"Improved default octaves for new bass vs melodic tracks.\" That is the whole official statement. {type:'gap'} Sources do not state the numeric default octaves (which C, which number). Do not invent C1 vs C3. Teach the fact that exists: a new bass track and a new melodic track are no longer assumed to start on the same octave after 1.2."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still control octave after the track exists. In Instrument View, <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> include an <strong>Octave</strong> submenu of buttons that change the octave of the Pad View. That control is per track and is saved with the Set. If a new bass feels too high or a lead feels too low, change <strong>Octave</strong> on that track. Do not assume the 1.2 default is a rule you cannot override."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1 and later, new melodic tracks default to a piano layout. That is a layout default, not an octave number. Keep the two facts separate."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Confirm you are on 1.2 or later for this default.","Add a bass-type track (or open the default bass Track Preset) and a melodic track.","Open each in Instrument View. Open <strong>Pad Performance Options → Octave</strong> and write which octave each track is on.","Change <strong>Octave</strong> on one track, return to Session View, reopen the track, and confirm the octave stuck.","Recite the sourced fact: Note 1.2 improved default octaves for new bass vs melodic tracks. Numeric values are not documented."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version improved default octaves for new bass vs melodic tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2.","Do the sources say the exact default octave numbers? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap.","How do you change octave after the track exists? <strong>Answer:</strong> Instrument View → <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Octave</strong>. The setting is per track and saved with the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.2: improved default octaves for new bass vs melodic tracks","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 Pad Performance Options (Octave submenu; per-track, saved with the Set)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: piano layout default for new melodic tracks (separate fact)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add one bass track and one melodic track. Write the octave each opens on. If they differ, that is the 1.2 improvement showing. If you need a different register, set <strong>Octave</strong> yourself."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-2-10","title":"2.2.10 — 5-track empty skeleton under max-8 rule","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.10 — 5-track empty skeleton under max-8 rule","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.2 — Tracks deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can build a five-track empty skeleton, stay under the eight-track cap, and leave three columns free."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is a lab. You are not capturing clips yet. You are building the Session columns you will fill later."},{"type":"prose","html":"Start from <strong>+ New</strong>. You already have three default tracks, each a random Track Preset (drum / bass / melodic / Sampler). Add two more tracks so the Set has five. On pre-2.0, swipe left and tap <strong>Add Track</strong> twice. On 2.0.1+, use <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> (and skip <strong>Audio</strong> unless you specifically want a 2.x audio column). Stop at five. The maximum is eight, so three slots remain for a later split, a shot, or a second drum kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Assign roles using only sourced tools: Track Preset type, column order, and Track Color on 1.7.2+. A practical five-role card from the beatmaking parts: drums, bass, harmony, lead, extra. {type:'gap'} There is no documented track-name field."},{"type":"prose","html":"Leave the clip grid empty. Empty squares are empty clip slots, not extra tracks. You will add clips in Module 2.3."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap <strong>+ New</strong>. Count three tracks.","Add tracks until you have five. Confirm you can still add more (you should have three adds left).","Long-press and reorder to drums, bass, harmony, lead, extra (match the closest presets).","On 1.7.2+, set <strong>Track Color</strong> per role. Open each header once to read the Track Preset name.","Look at the empty clip-slot grid. Confirm there are no clips. Recite: five tracks now, eight max, clips later."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many tracks should this skeleton have, and what is the hard maximum? <strong>Answer:</strong> Five now. Maximum eight.","How do you add the extra two tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe left → <strong>Add Track</strong> (pre-2.0), or <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> on 2.0.1+.","Should this lab contain clips? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Leave the empty slot grid empty."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1 (three default tracks; swipe left Add Track; max eight; long-press reorder)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (empty clip-slot grid)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 / §7.1 (drum / bass / melodic / Sampler; random Track Preset)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Beat / Synth / Audio); 1.7.2 (Track Color)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: kick, bass, chords, shots as role language"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save this five-track Set. Name it in Set Options so you can reuse it for clip and scene labs. Do not add a sixth track unless a role is truly missing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-2-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-2-3-103","code":"2.3","title":"2.3","blurb":"2.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l2-3-1","title":"2.3.1 — Empty slot grid","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.1 — Empty slot grid","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can read the empty squares under track headers as clip slots, not as extra tracks."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clips contain your captured loops and are the building blocks of your tracks. In a new Set you see an empty grid of squares beneath the track headers. Those squares are empty clip slots. That is where clips will live. They are not tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"A track is the vertical column. A slot is one cell in that column. Adding a clip fills a slot. Adding a track adds a new column of slots. Mixing those two ideas is how people think they hit the eight-track cap when they only made clips."},{"type":"prose","html":"The grid is also the scene map. Each horizontal row of slots is a scene once clips sit in it. Empty slots in a row mean that track stays silent if you launch that scene. You will use that on purpose later (sparse scenes)."},{"type":"prose","html":"You have at most eight tracks and eight scenes. Each clip, once it exists, is at most 16 bars. The empty grid already shows you those limits as a table. Do not fill it just to fill it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new Set or your five-track skeleton.","Point at one track header, then at the squares under it. Say \"one track, many slots.\"","Point across one row of squares. Say \"one future scene.\"","Confirm the squares are empty. Do not tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> yet.","Recite from memory: empty squares = clip slots; columns = tracks; rows = scenes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the empty squares under a track header? <strong>Answer:</strong> Empty clip slots.","Does filling a slot add a track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It adds a clip on the existing track.","What do empty slots in a row mean when you later launch that scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> That track has no clip in the scene, so it does not play a part there."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (empty grid of squares; clip slots)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks; §4.3 Scenes","Note FAQ: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a screenshot of the empty grid, circle one column and label it TRACK. Circle one row and label it SCENE. Circle one square and label it SLOT."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-2","title":"2.3.2 — Add Clip icon → Instrument View + new clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.2 — Add Clip icon → Instrument View + new clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> to create a clip and land in Instrument View, then return to Session View and see that clip in its slot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To add a clip, tap the <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon in a track. Adding a new clip takes you to Instrument View, where you record performances on the pads, edit recorded notes, and adjust instrument and audio effect parameters."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is different from tapping the track header. The header opens Instrument View without creating a clip. <strong>Add Clip</strong> does both: new clip plus Instrument View. The manual also lists a third door: double-tap an existing clip to open Instrument View on that clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once a clip exists, you are not required to capture notes immediately. Capture (Fixed Length and Free Length) is Level 5. In this lesson you only create the container and look at it."},{"type":"prose","html":"To get back to Session View, tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name. The new clip sits in the slot you tapped. If you later want another clip on the same track, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> in a different empty slot, or use <strong>Duplicate</strong> (next lessons)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open your five-track skeleton (or a new Set).","On the drum track, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> in the top empty slot.","Confirm Instrument View opened. Do not capture notes yet.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name. Confirm Session View shows a clip in that slot.","Tap the track header of a different track (not <strong>Add Clip</strong>). Confirm Instrument View opens and no new clip appeared. Return and recap the difference from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Add Clip</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It creates a new clip and opens Instrument View.","How is that different from tapping the track header? <strong>Answer:</strong> The header opens Instrument View without creating a clip.","How do you return to Session View after <strong>Add Clip</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (Add Clip icon; Instrument View; back arrow)","Ableton Note Manual §6 Instrument View (Add Clip also creates a new clip; header and double-tap are the other entries)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add one empty clip on drums and one on bass using <strong>Add Clip</strong>. Leave the other tracks empty. You will launch and stop these clips in the next lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-3","title":"2.3.3 — Launch on nearest downbeat","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.3 — Launch on nearest downbeat","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can tap a captured clip and know it starts on the nearest downbeat, not in the middle of a beat."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap on a captured clip to start its playback on the nearest downbeat. That is the launch rule. You do not get a separate quantization menu for Session launch in the sources. The clip waits for the next downbeat, then plays."},{"type":"prose","html":"\"Captured\" matters. An empty clip you just created with <strong>Add Clip</strong> has no notes yet. Launching it still starts transport, but you will not hear a part until notes exist. Capture is taught in Level 5. For this lesson, put a few notes into the clip (play pads and use <strong>Add</strong> on a Fixed Length clip, or play and <strong>Capture</strong> with transport stopped) so you can hear the downbeat."},{"type":"prose","html":"If other clips are already playing, the new tap still waits for the nearest downbeat before that clip joins. That is how you stack parts in time without an Arrangement View. Scene launch (Module 2.4) starts every clip in the row the same way, together."},{"type":"prose","html":"Global <strong>Play</strong> starts transport. Clip launch also starts playback. Use clip launch when you want a specific part to enter on the downbeat."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that has at least a short captured pattern (or capture one bar of pads now so you have something to hear).","Make sure transport is stopped. Tap the clip. Listen for it to start on the beat.","While it plays, tap a second captured clip on another track. Listen for it to join on the next downbeat.","Tap global <strong>Stop</strong>. Tap the first clip again and confirm launch still waits for the downbeat.","Recite the rule: tap a captured clip → playback starts on the nearest downbeat."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When you tap a captured clip, when does it start? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the nearest downbeat.","Does launching a clip require the global Play button first? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Tapping the clip starts playback.","If another clip is already playing, when does the new clip join? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the nearest downbeat."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (tap a captured clip; nearest downbeat)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2 (how a clip gets notes via Capture)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (global Play/Stop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Launch clip A, then clip B, then stop. Repeat until you can predict the downbeat join without watching the icon."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-4","title":"2.3.4 — Stop Clip control","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.4 — Stop Clip control","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can stop one playing clip with the square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon without treating that as global Stop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When a clip is playing, you see a square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon, which you use to stop that clip's playback. That icon belongs to the playing clip. It is not the global <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong> button in the bottom bar."},{"type":"prose","html":"Global <strong>Stop</strong> stops the Set. <strong>Stop Clip</strong> stops that clip. Other clips can keep playing. That is how you thin a scene without killing transport: hats stay, bass drops out, or the reverse."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pre-2.0 manual is the source for the square icon on a playing clip. Note 2.0.1 overhauled stop-button interactions so stop buttons appear on playing clips, and tapping a clip again while stop has been triggered re-triggers the clip. That 2.x behavior is the next lesson. On any version, learn this first: playing clip → square stop → that clip ends."},{"type":"prose","html":"If no clip is playing, there is no <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon to tap. Launch first, then stop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch one captured clip. Find the square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon on that clip.","Tap <strong>Stop Clip</strong>. Confirm that clip stopped. If other clips were silent, transport may also go quiet.","Launch two clips. Tap <strong>Stop Clip</strong> on only one. Confirm the other is still playing.","Tap global <strong>Stop</strong>. Confirm everything stopped.","Recite the difference: <strong>Stop Clip</strong> = this clip; bottom-bar <strong>Stop</strong> = the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is <strong>Stop Clip</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the playing clip. It is a square icon.","Does <strong>Stop Clip</strong> stop the whole Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It stops that clip's playback.","What stops the whole Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> The global <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong> button in the Session View bottom bar."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (square Stop Clip icon)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 Session View Actions (global Play/Stop)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: stop-button overhaul (next lesson)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With two clips playing, stop only the second clip, then relaunch it on the downbeat, then global Stop. Repeat until the two stop controls are automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-5","title":"2.3.5 — 2.x stop-button interaction improvements","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.5 — 2.x stop-button interaction improvements","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+ you can use the overhauled stop buttons, including re-triggering a clip after stop has been triggered."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1 overhauled interactions with stop buttons, which now appear on playing clips. Tapping a clip again while stop has been triggered re-triggers the clip. Those two sentences are the official 2.x change."},{"type":"prose","html":"Read them against the pre-2.0 manual. The older surface already showed a square <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon on a playing clip. 2.0.1 is an interaction overhaul, not the invention of stopping. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If you are below 2.0.1, use Lesson 2.3.4 only."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical reason to care is recoverability. If you tap stop by mistake, a second tap on that clip brings it back (re-trigger) instead of leaving a hole until the next scene. Launch still happens on the nearest downbeat."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent extra 2.x stop modes. The release notes do not describe a double-tap, a hold, or a fade. They describe stop buttons on playing clips, and a re-trigger if you tap again after stop is triggered."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If the version is below 2.0.1, write \"stop overhaul not on this install\" and stop here.","Launch a captured clip. Confirm a stop button is visible on the playing clip.","Tap stop so stop is triggered, then tap the same clip again before you need a different clip. Confirm it re-triggers.","Stop the clip and leave it stopped. Launch a different clip. Confirm this is ordinary launch, not the re-trigger case.","Recite the 2.0.1 rule from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 2.0.1 change about stop? <strong>Answer:</strong> Overhauled stop-button interactions; stop buttons now appear on playing clips.","What happens if you tap the clip again while stop has been triggered? <strong>Answer:</strong> The clip re-triggers.","Is this the first version that can stop a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The pre-2.0 manual already has a square Stop Clip icon. 2.0.1 overhauls the interaction."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Improvements: stop buttons on playing clips; tap again while stop triggered re-triggers the clip","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (pre-2.0 Stop Clip icon)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, play a two-clip groove. Trigger stop on one clip, then re-trigger it on the next downbeat. Repeat until the recover tap is reliable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-6","title":"2.3.6 — Long-press move across tracks/slots","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.6 — Long-press move across tracks/slots","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can long-press a clip and drag it to another slot on the same track or on a different track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can long-press a clip and then drag to move it to another clip slot on any track. \"Any track\" is the important word. The clip is not glued to the column where you created it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Moving a clip does not add a track and does not add a scene. It only changes which slot holds that clip. If you drop it on a slot that already has a clip, {type:'gap'} sources do not describe whether the destination is replaced, swapped, or rejected. Do not assume. Prefer empty slots when you practice."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use move to fix structure. A bass idea that landed in scene 1 can move to scene 2 (another row on the same track). A part you captured on the wrong column can move to the role-colored track you meant. You still have at most eight tracks and eight scenes, so you are rearranging inside the same table."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same long-press also opens <strong>Edit Clip</strong>, <strong>Duplicate</strong>, and <strong>Delete</strong>. Those are the next lesson. This lesson is the drag."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create or select a captured clip on the first track, top slot.","Long-press the clip until it lifts. Drag it to an empty slot on the same track (a lower row). Release.","Long-press again. Drag it to an empty slot on a different track. Release.","Drag it back to the original track and row.","Recite: long-press and drag moves a clip to any track's slot."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you move a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long-press it and drag it to another clip slot.","Can the destination be on a different track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The manual says any track.","Does moving a clip create a new track or scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only changes slots."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (long-press drag to another clip slot on any track)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 / §4.3 (8-track and 8-scene limits still apply)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Move one drum clip through three slots: same track down a row, across to another track, then home. Listen after each drop so you hear which column now plays that loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-7","title":"2.3.7 — Edit Clip / Duplicate / Delete","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.7 — Edit Clip / Duplicate / Delete","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can long-press a clip and choose <strong>Edit Clip</strong>, <strong>Duplicate</strong>, or <strong>Delete</strong> on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press on clips to access <strong>Edit Clip</strong>, <strong>Duplicate</strong>, and <strong>Delete</strong>. Those three commands sit on the same press as the move-drag. Lift to drag. Stay put to use the commands."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Edit Clip</strong> opens the clip for editing. Instrument View is also available by double-tapping the clip or using the track header. {type:'gap'} The manual names <strong>Edit Clip</strong> but does not spell out every screen it opens beyond the clip/Instrument View editing path. Use it as the Session-grid entry to edit that clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Duplicate</strong> copies the clip. In Instrument View there is also a <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> button that creates a duplicate in the next available clip slot and switches playback to that copy. Both are official. Session long-press Duplicate is the grid version. Use it to make variation B without recapturing variation A."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Delete</strong> removes the clip from its slot. The slot becomes empty again. The track remains. Use <strong>Undo</strong> if you delete the wrong clip. Auto-save is every 10–15 seconds, so undo immediately."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicating does not raise the 16-bar clip ceiling and does not add a track. It consumes another slot. You still have at most eight scene rows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Long-press a captured clip. Tap <strong>Duplicate</strong>. Confirm a second clip appears in another slot on that track (or the next available slot).","Long-press the copy. Tap <strong>Edit Clip</strong> (or double-tap the clip) and confirm you are in the clip's edit/Instrument View. Return to Session View.","Long-press the copy. Tap <strong>Delete</strong>. Confirm that slot is empty and the original clip remains.","Use <strong>Undo</strong> once, then <strong>Redo</strong>, so you feel recovery.","Recite the three commands from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where are Edit Clip, Duplicate, and Delete for a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the long-press menu on the clip.","Does Duplicate create a new track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It copies the clip into another slot.","What should you do right after a mistaken Delete? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap <strong>Undo</strong> in the Session View bottom bar."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (long-press Edit Clip, Duplicate, Delete)","Ableton Note Manual §6.1 Instrument View Actions (Duplicate Clip button → next available slot)","Ableton Note Manual §6 (double-tap existing clip to open Instrument View)","Note FAQ: auto-save every 10–15 seconds"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate a drum clip, open the copy for edit, then delete the copy. Keep the original. You will use this pair again in the variation lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-8","title":"2.3.8 — Clip length ceiling 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.8 — Clip length ceiling 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You treat 16 bars as the hard maximum clip length and you choose shorter Fixed Length values on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ is plain: clips can be a maximum of 16 bars long. Note 1.0.15 added clips up to 16 bars and uneven clip lengths. The manual's Fixed Length Capture offers 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars when you tap the plus icon at the bottom of the clip area."},{"type":"prose","html":"An older Capture KB page lists Fixed Length as 1, 2, 4, or 8 bars. The later manual and FAQ include 16. Teach 16 as the ceiling. If your Help version is older than 1.0.15, you may not see 16 in the plus menu. Check the version."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sixteen bars is a ceiling, not a target. A one-bar or two-bar drum loop is legal and often clearer. Free Length Capture with transport stopped lets you play up to eight bars and then tap <strong>Capture</strong>. Newly captured clips with transport running have at least as many bars as the shortest clip already in the Set. None of those paths beat the 16-bar maximum."},{"type":"prose","html":"You will edit loop length later in the MIDI Editor (loop brace, crop, duplicate). That editor still cannot grow a clip past 16 bars."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the version.","Tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> on an empty slot. In Instrument View, tap the plus icon in the clip area.","Read the offered lengths. Expect 1, 2, 4, 8, and (1.0.15+) 16. Pick 2 bars for this drill.","Return to Session View. Recite the FAQ rule: maximum 16 bars.","Write one sentence: \"I will pick the shortest length that holds the idea, never more than 16.\""],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the maximum clip length? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16 bars.","Which Fixed Length sizes does the manual list? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars.","Which release added clips up to 16 bars and uneven lengths? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ: maximum clip length 16 bars","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: clips up to 16 bars; uneven clip lengths","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 Fixed Length Capture (1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars)","Capture in Note KB: older Fixed Length list 1, 2, 4, or 8 (superseded for the 16-bar ceiling)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create three empty clips at 1, 2, and 4 bars (plus one at 16 if your version offers it). You will fill two of them as variations in the next labs."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-9","title":"2.3.9 — Variation stacks (clip A/B/C down a track)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.9 — Variation stacks (clip A/B/C down a track)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can stack clip A, B, and C down one track as variations of the same part."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A track is a vertical column of slots. Down that column you keep versions of one part. Across a row you keep the parts that play together. Variation stacks use the vertical axis."},{"type":"prose","html":"Make clip A first. Duplicate it into the next slot (Session long-press <strong>Duplicate</strong>, or the Instrument View <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> button, which copies into the next available slot and switches playback to the copy). That copy is clip B. Duplicate again for clip C. You now have three slots on one track. Change only B and C later (hats, velocity, a fill). Do not recapture A if A is the version you trust."},{"type":"prose","html":"You have at most eight scene rows, so a stack of three uses three of those rows on this track. Other tracks can stay empty in those rows until you build scenes. Each clip still maxes out at 16 bars."},{"type":"prose","html":"Launch one clip at a time on this track. Only one clip per track plays. Tapping B starts B on the nearest downbeat and replaces A. That is how you audition variations without an Arrangement View."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the drum track, get one captured clip in the top slot. Call it A.","Long-press A and tap <strong>Duplicate</strong> (or use <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> in Instrument View). Call the copy B.","Duplicate B to make C. Confirm three clips sit down the same track.","Launch A, then B, then C. Listen for the downbeat switch. Stop.","Recite: one track, stacked variations; launch swaps the playing clip on that track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why stack clips down one track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Those slots are variations of one part. Rows are scenes; columns are tracks.","How do you make B without recapturing A? <strong>Answer:</strong> Duplicate the clip (Session long-press or Instrument View Duplicate Clip).","Can A and B on the same track play at the same time? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Launching B replaces A on that track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1–4.3 (vertical tracks, clip slots, horizontal scenes)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (Duplicate on long-press; launch on nearest downbeat)","Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (Duplicate Clip → next available slot and switch playback)","Note FAQ: 8 scenes; 16-bar clip maximum"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave A untouched. You will change only hats on B in the next lesson's lab (or after Capture if B is still empty)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-3-10","title":"2.3.10 — two variations on one drum track","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.10 — two variations on one drum track","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.3 — Clips deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can put two drum variations on one track and switch them on the downbeat."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab uses everything in Module 2.3. One drum track. Clip A is the base loop. Clip B is the variation. They sit in two slots down the same column."},{"type":"prose","html":"Build A first (Add Clip → capture a short pattern, 1 or 2 bars is enough, never more than 16). Duplicate A to the next slot to make B. Change only B. The syllabus practice is: duplicate a clip and change only hats later (after Capture). If you cannot edit individual hits yet, still keep B as a separate clip — even an empty extra slot is the wrong idea. B must be a real second clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"Launch A. Then launch B. B starts on the nearest downbeat and replaces A on that track. Stop B with <strong>Stop Clip</strong> (and on 2.0.1+, practice the re-trigger tap). Do not use a second drum track for the variation. A second track would count toward eight and would play at the same time as A if you launched both."},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty slots on other tracks can wait. This lab is one column, two clips."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the drum track, create clip A and capture a short drum idea (1–2 bars).","Duplicate A into the next slot. That copy is B.","Open B and change only the hats (add or remove hat notes). Leave kicks and snares as they are if you can.","In Session View, launch A, then B, then A again. Confirm the switch lands on the downbeat.","Stop with <strong>Stop Clip</strong>, then global <strong>Stop</strong>. Recite: two variations, one track, 16-bar ceiling, eight-scene table."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why do both variations live on one track? <strong>Answer:</strong> They are versions of the same part. Two drum tracks would play together and spend an extra column.","When does B start after you tap it? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the nearest downbeat, replacing A on that track.","What is the clip length ceiling you must respect on A and B? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16 bars."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (Add Clip, launch, Stop Clip, long-press Duplicate)","Ableton Note Manual §6.1 Duplicate Clip","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (Fixed Length 1/2/4/8/16)","Note FAQ: 16-bar maximum; 8 tracks","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: hi-hat as the place you vary divisions"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep A as the verse groove and B as the hat variation. You will place them on different scenes in Module 2.4."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-3-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-2-4-104","code":"2.4","title":"2.4","blurb":"2.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l2-4-1","title":"2.4.1 — A scene is a horizontal row of clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.1 — A scene is a horizontal row of clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can point at a horizontal row and call it a scene: the clips that play together."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Scenes are made up from various clips that are on the same horizontal row. That is the definition. A scene is not a timeline region and not an Arrangement block. It is one row of the Session grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can still play individual clips from different scenes by tapping them during playback. The scene is the row you can also launch as a group. Launching the row is the next lessons. First you have to see the row."},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty slots in that row are part of the scene. They simply have no clip, so that track stays silent if you launch the scene. A scene with one clip is still a scene. A scene with a clip on every track is still a scene. The row is the unit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Each Set can have at most eight scenes. You do not need eight. One full row is enough to start. Beatmaking advice is to avoid only spinning an 8-bar loop that goes nowhere — but that loop is a great place to start. Scenes are how that loop becomes sections."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show scene numbers with <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> so the left of each row is numbered.","Point across the top numbered row. Name every clip (or empty slot) in that row.","Point down one track and name those clips as variations, not as a scene.","Hide scene numbers, then show them again and find the same row.","Recite: a scene is the clips on one horizontal row."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is a scene in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> The clips that sit on the same horizontal row.","Can a scene include empty slots? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Those tracks simply have no clip in that row.","How many scenes can a Set have? <strong>Answer:</strong> At most eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (horizontal row; max eight)","Note FAQ: up to eight scenes","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: start with a loop, then make sections"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On your lab Set, circle scene 1 (the top row) on a screenshot. Write the role of each clip in that row. Leave lower rows for later scenes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-2","title":"2.4.2 — Show/Hide scene numbers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.2 — Show/Hide scene numbers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can show scene numbers when you need to launch rows, and hide them when you want the grid."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To display the scene numbers, tap the <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> icon at the bottom of the Session View grid. That control is one of the four Session View Actions, next to <strong>Undo</strong>/<strong>Redo</strong>, the mixer, and global <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"You tap a scene number to launch all clips in that scene. If the numbers are hidden, you cannot tap them. Show the numbers when you are arranging or performing sections. Hide them when you are dragging clips or want a larger grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"The numbers are not extra scenes. They label the rows that already exist. Hiding them does not delete scenes. Showing them does not add scenes. You still have at most eight."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a small iPhone screen this toggle is easy to miss. Learn its place in the bottom bar so you are not hunting during a launch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Look at the Session View bottom bar. Point to <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong>.","Tap it so numbers appear at the left of each row. Count the visible rows.","Tap it again so the numbers hide. Confirm the clips are still in those rows.","Show the numbers again. Tap nothing else. Just rest your thumb on that icon.","Recite: bottom bar toggle shows or hides scene numbers; it does not create or delete scenes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Show/Hide Scene Numbers? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Session View bottom bar (Session View Actions).","Why show the numbers? <strong>Answer:</strong> So you can tap a scene number and launch every clip in that row.","Does hiding scene numbers delete the scenes? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (Show/Hide Scene Numbers icon)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 Session View Actions"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hide the numbers, add or move one clip, then show the numbers and confirm the row still matches what you expect. Repeat until the toggle is automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-3","title":"2.4.3 — Launch scene vs single clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.3 — Launch scene vs single clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can launch a whole row with the scene number, or launch single clips from different scenes during playback."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play individual clips from different scenes by tapping them during playback. Or you can launch a scene itself by tapping on the scene number, which will play all clips in the selected scene. Those are the two launch modes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scene launch is the section button. One tap, every clip in that row starts (on the nearest downbeat, same as a single clip). Empty slots in the row stay silent. Single-clip launch is the override. You can start a lead from scene 3 while drums from scene 1 are still playing, because you tapped the lead clip, not scene 3."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use scene launch when the row is the section you want. Use single-clip launch when you want a hybrid that is not stored as its own scene. You only have eight scenes, so hybrids by hand save rows."},{"type":"prose","html":"Global <strong>Play</strong> starts transport. It does not pick a scene. Scene numbers pick the row. <strong>Stop Clip</strong> still stops one clip. Global <strong>Stop</strong> stops the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Put at least one clip in scene 1 on two tracks, and a different clip in scene 2 on one of those tracks.","Show scene numbers. Tap scene 1. Confirm both scene-1 clips start.","While it plays, tap one clip that lives in scene 2. Confirm that one clip joins or replaces on its track, and the rest of scene 1 can keep going.","Tap scene 2. Confirm the whole row takes over.","Recite: scene number = whole row; clip tap = that clip only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you launch every clip in a row? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the scene number (show scene numbers first).","Can you play clips from different scenes at once? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Tap individual clips during playback.","Does global Play launch a scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It starts transport. Scene numbers launch rows."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (individual clips from different scenes, or tap scene number)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (clip launch on nearest downbeat; Stop Clip)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (global Play/Stop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Perform a 60-second pass: scene 1, then one clip from scene 2, then scene 2, then Stop Clip on the extra part, then global Stop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-4","title":"2.4.4 — Max 8 scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.4 — Max 8 scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You treat eight scenes as a hard cap and you spend those rows on sections, not on unused copies."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Each Set can have up to a maximum of eight scenes. The FAQ says the same: Note Sets can have up to eight scenes. That number matches the eight-track cap as a square table: 8 × 8 slots, each clip at most 16 bars."},{"type":"prose","html":"Eight is a ceiling. You do not owe the Set eight sections. Beatmaking advice is to start with a loop, then add a chorus/hook, a sparser verse, and an intro/outro. That is four rows if you use one row per section. You still have four in reserve."},{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press a scene number to duplicate a scene (next lesson). Each duplicate spends a row. Duplicating to \"try something\" seven times will fill the cap. Delete scenes you are not using."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not document a separate \"Add Scene\" button. New rows appear as you place clips lower in the grid and as you duplicate scenes. Do not invent a ninth row."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show scene numbers. Count the rows you actually use in this Set.","Write \"8 scenes max\" next to your role card.","Plan no more than four section names for now (hook, verse, intro, outro — sourced names). Leave four rows free.","If you already duplicated scenes past what you need, long-press a spare scene number and delete it (next lesson's command).","Recite: eight scenes maximum; spend them on sections."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the maximum number of scenes? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8.","Is eight a target? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a ceiling. A few sections are enough.","Does duplicating a scene count toward eight? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (maximum of eight scenes)","Note FAQ: up to eight scenes","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: chorus/hook, verse, intro/outro from a starting loop"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"List the eight scene slots on paper. Assign at most four. Leave the rest blank on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-5","title":"2.4.5 — Long-press rearrange / duplicate / delete scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.5 — Long-press rearrange / duplicate / delete scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can long-press a scene number to drag the row, duplicate it, or delete it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press on a scene number to drag and rearrange it, as well as access duplicate and delete commands. You must show scene numbers first. There is no scene handle if the numbers are hidden."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drag changes section order. If the hook is in row 1 and you want it in row 2, long-press the number and drag. The clips in that row move with it. Other rows shift."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate copies the whole row — every clip in that scene — into another scene slot. That is the fastest way to make a verse from a hook: duplicate, then delete or stop the extra parts. Each duplicate counts toward eight scenes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Delete removes that scene. Use <strong>Undo</strong> if you hit the wrong number. Deleting a scene does not delete the tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"These three commands match the track long-press pattern (reorder / Duplicate / Delete) and the clip long-press pattern (move / Duplicate / Delete). Same grammar, three objects: track, clip, scene."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show scene numbers. Build or keep at least one row that has two clips.","Long-press that scene number and drag it one row down. Confirm the clips moved with it.","Long-press again and tap duplicate. Confirm a second scene now holds the same clips. Count scenes toward eight.","Long-press the copy and delete it. Confirm the original row remains.","Recite the three scene-number commands from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you long-press to rearrange, duplicate, or delete a scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> The scene number (after you show scene numbers).","What does duplicating a scene copy? <strong>Answer:</strong> The whole row — the clips in that scene.","Does deleting a scene delete the tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It removes that row of clips."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (long-press scene number: drag, duplicate, delete)","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 (max eight scenes)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (Undo/Redo)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate scene 1, drag the copy below it, then delete the copy. Repeat until the long-press menu is as familiar as the track menu."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-6","title":"2.4.6 — Assign scenes to song sections","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.6 — Assign scenes to song sections","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can treat each scene as a song section using only section names the sources give you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note does not name scenes \"verse\" or \"hook\" in the UI. {type:'gap'} Sources do not document scene titles. You assign meaning. The Beatmaking article is the method source."},{"type":"prose","html":"Start with an 8-bar loop (or shorter — clip max is 16, not a required 8). Then make sections. The article names these: chorus or hook (memorable melody and rhythm), verse (storytelling; keep instrumentation sparse so you can hear vocals), and intro/outro (how you get in and how you get out; introduce a new instrument every 1, 2, or 4 bars, keep it short). {type:'gap'} \"Break\" is in the syllabus form template but not in the Beatmaking source. Do not teach Break as an official Note section. If you want a breakdown, treat it as a sparse verse or an outro row and say so."},{"type":"prose","html":"You have eight scene rows. A practical first map: scene 1 = hook (full idea), scene 2 = verse (sparser), scene 3 = intro or outro. Duplicate the hook row, then remove clips to make the verse. That is the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write the section name on paper or in the Set name. The grid will not label it for you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show scene numbers on your lab Set.","Write three lines: Scene 1 = hook, Scene 2 = verse, Scene 3 = intro or outro.","Put the fullest idea in scene 1. Duplicate it to scene 2 (you will thin it in 2.4.7).","Leave scene 3 empty or hold one entering part for an intro.","Recite the sourced section list: hook/chorus, verse, intro/outro. Eight scenes max."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does Note store scene titles like \"Verse\"? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources do not document scene titles. You assign the meaning.","Which section names does Beatmaking give you? <strong>Answer:</strong> Chorus/hook, verse, intro/outro.","Why start with the hook row? <strong>Answer:</strong> Beatmaking: start with the chorus or hook; it should be memorable. Duplicate it to make other sections."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note: chorus/hook, verse (sparse for vocals), intro/outro","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 (scenes are rows; max eight; duplicate via long-press)","Note FAQ: eight scenes"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On paper, map scenes 1–3 to hook / verse / intro. Do not fill scenes 4–8 yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-7","title":"2.4.7 — Sparse scenes versus full scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.7 — Sparse scenes versus full scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can make a full scene and a sparse scene from the same idea by leaving slots empty."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A full scene has clips on most tracks in that row — drums, bass, harmony, lead. A sparse scene is the same row idea with fewer clips. Empty slots are legal. When you launch that scene, tracks with empty slots stay silent."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking says keep verse instrumentation sparse so you can always hear the vocals. It also says introduce a new instrument every 1, 2, or 4 bars in an intro. Those are energy rules, not extra Note features. You create the energy change by which slots you fill."},{"type":"prose","html":"How to build the pair: duplicate the full (hook) scene, then delete the clips you do not want in the verse — often the lead, sometimes harmony. Do not mute as your only arrangement method yet. Mute is a mixer listen tool (Module 2.5). Arrangement that survives export is which clips sit in the row. Export (Level 3 / 11) records scenes top to bottom; empty slots in a scene stay empty in the bounce."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can also play a hybrid by launching single clips from another scene. Store the versions you care about as real rows so you can launch them in one tap."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Start from a row that has at least drums, bass, and one more part. Call it the full scene.","Long-press its scene number and duplicate it.","In the copy, delete the lead (or the busiest part) so drums and bass remain. That is the sparse scene.","Launch the full scene, then the sparse scene. Listen for the drop in density.","Recite: full = more clips in the row; sparse = empty slots on purpose."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What makes a scene sparse? <strong>Answer:</strong> Empty clip slots in that row, so those tracks do not play when you launch the scene.","Why keep a verse sparse, according to Beatmaking? <strong>Answer:</strong> So you can always hear the vocals.","Should you rely on mute alone to define a section you will export? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Export follows scenes and the clips in them. Put the thin version in its own row."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 (row of clips; launch scene; empty slots simply have no clip)","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 (export is scenes top to bottom; longest clip in the scene sets length)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: sparse verse; intro by adding instruments"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make scene 1 full and scene 2 drums+bass only. Launch each twice. That pair is the core of the next lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-4-8","title":"2.4.8 — three scenes from one 4-track idea","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.8 — three scenes from one 4-track idea","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.4 — Scenes deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn one four-track idea into three launchable scenes without adding a fifth track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab is the syllabus practice: scene 1 full; scene 2 drums+bass only; scene 3 add lead. Use four tracks (drums, bass, harmony, lead). Stay under eight tracks and eight scenes. Clips stay at or under 16 bars."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture or reuse one idea across the four tracks in scene 1 so the row is full. Duplicate scene 1 to scene 2 and delete harmony and lead so only drums and bass remain. Duplicate scene 1 (or scene 2) to scene 3 and keep drums, bass, and lead — or add the lead clip into scene 3 if you built scene 3 from the sparse row. The point is three stored rows, not three extra tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Launch with scene numbers: 1 (full), 2 (sparse), 3 (lead returns). Then try a hybrid: leave scene 2 running and tap the lead clip from scene 3. Hear the difference between a stored section and a one-hand override."},{"type":"prose","html":"You now have arrangement without Arrangement View. Later, Export Audio will play these scenes top to bottom. Put them in the order you want the bounce to have (long-press drag if the hook should not be first)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On four tracks, fill scene 1 with drums, bass, harmony, and lead.","Duplicate scene 1 to scene 2. Delete harmony and lead in scene 2.","Build scene 3 so it has drums, bass, and lead (duplicate scene 1 and delete only harmony, or copy the lead into the sparse row).","Show scene numbers. Launch 1, then 2, then 3. Stop.","Recite the three rows from memory: full; drums+bass; add lead."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many tracks does this lab use, and what is the maximum? <strong>Answer:</strong> Four tracks. Maximum eight.","What is in each of the three scenes? <strong>Answer:</strong> Scene 1 full (four parts). Scene 2 drums+bass. Scene 3 adds the lead back (no need for a fifth track).","How do you launch each section in one tap? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the scene number."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.3 (scenes, launch, duplicate, max eight)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1–4.2 (tracks, clips, 8-track cap)","Note FAQ: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: hook vs sparse verse; add instruments for intro energy","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 (export order is scenes top to bottom)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Perform the three scenes for one minute. Write pass/fail: four tracks, three scenes, one-tap launches. This Set is the one you will mix in Module 2.5."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-4-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-2-5-105","code":"2.5","title":"2.5","blurb":"2.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l2-5-1","title":"2.5.1 — Show/Hide mixer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.1 — Show/Hide mixer","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open and close the mixer from the Session View bottom bar and find mute, solo, and volume there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You access the mixer for your Set by tapping the <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> icon at the bottom of the Session View grid. That icon is one of the four Session View Actions, with <strong>Undo</strong>/<strong>Redo</strong>, scene numbers, and <strong>Play</strong>/<strong>Stop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"When the mixer is shown, you get per-track <strong>M</strong> (mute), <strong>S</strong> (solo), and volume sliders. When it is hidden, those controls are not on the clip grid. Showing the mixer does not change the music by itself. It only reveals the strip."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.0.15 and later, the Main channel is part of this mixer world: it controls system volume, and you can put up to two audio effects on Main. Note 1.3 also says new Sets include default effects on the Main track. Those are later lessons in this module. First, find the toggle."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hide the mixer when you need the grid for launching. Show it when you balance, mute, or solo. The toggle is not a mix bus. It is a visibility switch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open your four-track, three-scene lab Set.","Tap <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> in the bottom bar. Confirm <strong>M</strong>, <strong>S</strong>, and volume sliders appear on the tracks.","Hide the mixer. Confirm the clip grid is back and the sound did not change.","Show the mixer again. Leave it open for the next lessons.","Recite: bottom bar <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> reveals mute, solo, and volume."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the mixer toggle? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> icon at the bottom of the Session View grid.","What appears when the mixer is shown? <strong>Answer:</strong> Per-track mute (M), solo (S), and volume sliders.","Does hiding the mixer bypass mute or volume? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources describe it as show/hide. Hide it to see the grid; your last mute/volume settings remain the mix. Do not treat hide as \"reset.\""],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (Show/Hide Mixer)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 Session View Actions","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Main channel; up to 2 Main FX (later lessons)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Show mixer, hide mixer, show mixer — ten times — until your thumb hits the icon without looking."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-2","title":"2.5.2 — Mute (M)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.2 — Mute (M)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can mute and unmute a track with <strong>M</strong> and hear that track disappear from the mix."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With the mixer shown, tap <strong>M</strong> to mute a track. A muted track stays in the Set. Its clips are still there. You just do not hear it until you tap <strong>M</strong> again."},{"type":"prose","html":"Mute is a listen and isolate tool. It is not a scene. If you want a section without the lead, put that version in its own scene (empty lead slot). Mute is for \"I need to hear the kick without the hat\" while you work, or for silencing a track you are not ready to delete."},{"type":"prose","html":"Mute is per track, not per clip. Muting drums silences every clip on that column. To silence one clip only, use <strong>Stop Clip</strong> or leave that slot empty in the scene."},{"type":"prose","html":"You have at most eight tracks, so mute is also how you park a spare column without deleting it. Unmute when you need it. <strong>Undo</strong> reverses a mute if you hit the wrong <strong>M</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show the mixer. Launch a scene that has at least two parts playing.","Tap <strong>M</strong> on the lead (or the busiest track). Confirm it goes silent and the others continue.","Tap <strong>M</strong> again on that track. Confirm it returns.","Mute drums, then unmute. Notice you cannot hear a groove without the pulse — that is why drums stay in most scenes.","Recite: <strong>M</strong> mutes the whole track; it does not delete clips or change scenes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>M</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It mutes that track.","Does mute delete the track's clips? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The clips stay. You stop hearing the track.","Is mute the same as a sparse scene? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Sparse scenes use empty slots. Mute is a mixer listen control on the whole track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (tap M to mute a track)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2–4.3 (Stop Clip and empty scene slots are the arrangement alternatives)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With scene 1 playing, mute each of the four tracks in turn, then unmute. Stop when <strong>M</strong> is automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-3","title":"2.5.3 — Solo (S)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.3 — Solo (S)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can solo a track with <strong>S</strong> and hear that track alone, then release solo to return to the mix."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With the mixer shown, tap <strong>S</strong> to solo a track. Solo is the opposite listening move from mute. Mute hides one track. Solo isolates one track (or more, if you tap <strong>S</strong> on others). The manual shows both controls next to each other and does not add extra solo modes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use solo to judge a part. Bass tuning, hat pattern, whether the lead is actually in key — those questions are easier when the other columns are silent. Then turn solo off and listen in context. Beatmaking says not to spend too long fine-tuning an idea until you decide it is worth continuing. Solo is a short listen, not a mix destination."},{"type":"prose","html":"Solo is per track. It does not create a scene. Export follows scenes and clips, not your current solo state. {type:'gap'} Sources do not state whether solo is written into Export Audio. Do not bounce a \"solo mix\" by leaving <strong>S</strong> on and hoping. Make a scene that contains only the clips you want."},{"type":"prose","html":"If both <strong>M</strong> and <strong>S</strong> are available on the same strip, use one job at a time while you learn: solo to isolate, mute to hide."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show the mixer. Launch a full scene.","Tap <strong>S</strong> on drums. Confirm you hear drums alone.","Tap <strong>S</strong> on bass as well if a second solo engages (listen). Then turn solo off on both so the full mix returns.","Solo the lead for four bars, then release. Decide one thing about that part, then stop.","Recite: <strong>S</strong> isolates a track for listening; it is not a scene and not an export setting."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>S</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It solos that track.","Should you leave solo on to define an exported section? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Build a scene with the clips you want. Export follows scenes.","When is solo useful? <strong>Answer:</strong> Short listens to judge one part (tuning, pattern, density) before you return to the mix."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (tap S to solo a track)","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 (export is scenes top to bottom)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: do not over-fine-tune too early (80/20)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Solo each of the four lab tracks for one launch cycle, then release. Write one observation per track. Do not change volumes yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-4","title":"2.5.4 — Volume sliders","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.4 — Volume sliders","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can move a track's volume slider to set how loud that column is against the others."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With the mixer shown, use the sliders to adjust the track's volume. That is the whole official control. Each track has its own slider. Lower a part that masks the kick. Raise a lead you cannot hear. The musical reason is balance, not \"more is better.\""},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not publish target dB values or a default slider position. Do not invent −6 dB or unity-gain numbers. Use your ears. Beatmaking says kick and bass create the low-frequency push together — if they bury each other, pull one slider down. Hats and shots sit above that."},{"type":"prose","html":"Volume is per track. It applies to every clip on that column. If variation B should be quieter than variation A, that is not a slider job; that is a different clip (or later, velocity/clip gain on 2.x audio). Keep the slider as the role's level."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.0.15+, double-tap the volume fader to reset (next lesson). On 1.0.15+, Main controls system volume (the lesson after that). Device volume and Main are not substitutes for per-track sliders."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show the mixer. Launch the full scene.","Pull the lead slider down until the lead sits behind drums and bass. Listen.","Raise the lead until it is clear, then stop before it covers the kick.","Balance bass against drums the same way. Leave harmony in the middle.","Recite: sliders set per-track volume; no official target numbers; listen."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do the mixer sliders do? <strong>Answer:</strong> They adjust that track's volume.","Do sources give you target dB values? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap. Use your ears.","Does one slider change only the playing clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is the track's volume for every clip on that column."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (use the sliders to adjust the track's volume)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: kick and bass as the low-frequency push","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: double-tap volume fader to reset; Main channel system volume"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set a first balance on the four-track lab. Do not touch Main FX. Play scene 1 and scene 2 and confirm drums+bass still read when the lead is gone."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-5","title":"2.5.5 — Double-tap reset volume","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.5 — Double-tap reset volume","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.0.15+ you can double-tap a volume fader (or a parameter) to reset it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15 added: double-tap parameters or volume fader to reset. That is the official gesture. It is version-gated. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Below 1.0.15, drag the slider back by ear; there is no documented reset tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"Reset returns the fader (or parameter) to its default. {type:'gap'} Sources do not name the numeric default. You will hear the track jump back to the factory level of that control. Use reset when you have dragged a slider into a hole and want a known starting point, then balance again on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same double-tap works on parameters, not only volume. That is the \"parameter reset culture\" this lesson introduces. You will use it on devices and FX in later levels. Here, stay on the mixer fader so the gesture is clean."},{"type":"prose","html":"Double-tap is not Undo. Undo steps back one action. Double-tap reset jumps to default, even if you made five small moves."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If the version is below 1.0.15, write that reset is unavailable and skip the tap.","Show the mixer. Drag one volume slider far down. Listen.","Double-tap that fader. Confirm the level jumps back to default.","Set a deliberate balance again. Double-tap a different track by mistake, then redo that track's level.","Recite: Note 1.0.15+ double-tap volume fader (or a parameter) to reset."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version added double-tap reset? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15.","What can you double-tap to reset? <strong>Answer:</strong> Parameters or the volume fader.","Is double-tap reset the same as Undo? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Reset jumps to default. Undo reverses the last action."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15: double-tap parameters or volume fader to reset","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 (volume sliders)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (Undo/Redo)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ruin one fader on purpose, double-tap reset, then set the level once. Repeat on two tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-6","title":"2.5.6 — Main channel / system volume","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.6 — Main channel / system volume","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.0.15+ you can use the Main channel as the control that sets system volume."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15 added: Main channel controls system volume; up to 2 audio effects on main. The syllabus writes 1.0.16+. The release note that names the feature is <strong>1.0.15</strong>. Teach 1.0.15. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Main is not a ninth track. The track cap is still eight. Main is the output path for the Set. Moving Main changes how loud the whole Set is on the device, because it controls system volume. Per-track sliders still set the balance between drums, bass, harmony, and lead. Do not pull every track down if the phone is simply too loud — that is Main (and the device volume hardware)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The pre-2.0 manual chapter 4 does not describe the Main channel. The control lives in the mixer/Main area added in 1.0.15. If you cannot find Main, you are on an older version or the mixer is hidden. Show the mixer first."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.3 added default effects on the Main track for new Sets. Those are Main FX, the next lesson. This lesson is level only."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Confirm 1.0.15 or later.","Show the mixer. Find the <strong>Main</strong> channel (the output, not one of the eight tracks).","Launch a scene. Move Main down, then up. Confirm the whole Set changes loudness.","Leave per-track sliders where you set them in 2.5.4. Only Main should have moved.","Recite: Main (1.0.15+) controls system volume; track sliders still do the balance."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version made Main control system volume? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15 (not 1.0.16).","Is Main a ninth track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You still have at most eight tracks. Main is the output path.","When do you reach for Main instead of a track slider? <strong>Answer:</strong> When the whole Set is too loud or too quiet. Use track sliders to balance parts."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Main channel controls system volume; up to 2 audio effects on main","Note Release Notes 1.3: new Sets include default effects on the Main track","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 (per-track sliders)","Note FAQ: maximum 8 tracks"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Main so the Set is comfortable on your current listening path. Do not restack the four track sliders unless one part is still wrong."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-7","title":"2.5.7 — Main FX up to two (version-aware)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.7 — Main FX up to two (version-aware)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You know Main can hold up to two audio effects on Note 1.0.15+, and you check your version before you look for them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15: up to 2 audio effects on main. Note 1.3: new Sets include default effects on the Main track. Those two notes are the version story. The pre-2.0 Session chapter does not document Main FX. Treat this as a release-note feature."},{"type":"prose","html":"Two is the cap on Main. That is separate from the two audio effects that already live inside each Track Preset (instrument + two FX). Main FX process the whole Set after the tracks. Use them for a shared space (the manual's later effect list includes Reverb, Delay, Saturator, and others). {type:'gap'} Sources do not give a recommended Main FX pair. Do not invent \"always Reverb + Delay.\""},{"type":"prose","html":"If your version is older than 1.0.15, Main FX are not available. If you are on 1.3 or later, a new Set may already have default Main effects loaded — listen before you stack more. You still cannot exceed two on Main."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not the full effects level (Level 8). Here you only locate Main, count the slots, and leave the sketch's instruments doing most of the work. Beatmaking: do not spend too long fine-tuning until the idea is worth it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the version.","If you are on 1.0.15+, show the mixer / Main and find the Main effect slots (up to two).","If you are on 1.3+, check whether a new Set already loaded default Main effects. Listen with them in, then with them bypassed or cleared if the UI allows.","Do not add a third Main effect. Confirm the cap is two.","Recite the version gate: 1.0.15 up to two Main FX; 1.3 default Main effects on new Sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many audio effects can Main hold from 1.0.15? <strong>Answer:</strong> Up to two.","How is that different from Track Preset FX? <strong>Answer:</strong> Each Track Preset already has an instrument plus two audio effects. Main FX sit on the output and affect the whole Set.","What did Note 1.3 add? <strong>Answer:</strong> New Sets include default effects on the Main track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15: up to 2 audio effects on main","Note Release Notes 1.3: default effects on the Main track","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (Track Preset = instrument + two audio effects)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: 80/20 — do not over-fine-tune early"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 1.0.15+, identify the two Main FX slots and write what (if anything) is already loaded. Leave them or set one shared effect. Do not rebuild the track mix around Main."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-8","title":"2.5.8 — Balance for mobile speakers vs headphones","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.8 — Balance for mobile speakers vs headphones","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can check the same mix on your real listening path and prefer wired headphones when you play."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not give speaker-vs-headphone EQ curves or target slider numbers. Do not invent a \"phone speaker mix.\" Teach what is sourced."},{"type":"prose","html":"Bluetooth headphones have 34–200 ms of latency. Wired headphones average 5–10 ms. Ableton recommends wired headphones to play instruments in Note. Latency makes pad timing and Capture harder. It also makes you chase volume that is not really a mix problem. If the groove feels late on AirPods, that is codec delay, not a slider."},{"type":"prose","html":"The orange microphone indicator is expected when Note uses the mic. The screen stays awake while Note is in the foreground (1.0.15). Neither fact is a mix control."},{"type":"prose","html":"What you can do with official controls: set per-track sliders for balance, set Main (1.0.15+) for overall loudness, mute/solo to judge parts, and listen on the path you will actually use. Built-in speakers hide bass. Headphones reveal hats and bass together. If the idea only works on one path, say so — do not \"fix\" it with undocumented tricks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Prefer wired for playing and Capture. Use Bluetooth only when you must, and do not judge timing there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch your full scene on the device speakers at a modest Main level. Write what you can and cannot hear.","Plug in wired headphones if you have them. Listen again. Note kick/bass and hats.","If you only have Bluetooth, listen once, then take them off and remember the 34–200 ms range. Do not recapture on Bluetooth.","Adjust track sliders only if a part is truly missing on the path you will use. Do not chase Bluetooth delay with volume.","Recite: wired 5–10 ms, Bluetooth 34–200 ms; prefer wired; no official speaker EQ recipe."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What latency ranges do the sources give? <strong>Answer:</strong> Bluetooth 34–200 ms; wired 5–10 ms.","What does Ableton recommend for playing instruments? <strong>Answer:</strong> Wired headphones.","Is there an official speaker-vs-headphone mix recipe? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap. Use sliders, Main, and your ears on the real path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Bluetooth Headphones and Latency in Note: 34–200 ms Bluetooth; 5–10 ms wired; use wired","Note FAQ: recommend wired headphones to play instruments","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Main channel system volume; screen stays awake in foreground","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 (M, S, sliders)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write two lines: \"On speakers I hear ___.\" \"On wired headphones I hear ___.\" Adjust one slider if needed. Stop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l2-5-9","title":"2.5.9 — Gate B assessment","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.9 — Gate B assessment","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 2 · Module 2.5 — Mixer deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can self-score Gate B from Level 2 facts: a Set with enough tracks and scenes, mixer used on purpose, clip launch and stop fluent."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate B is a checklist you score yourself. The academy app cannot watch Note. Use your own Set, not a Demo."},{"type":"prose","html":"Required bar, from this level's sourced facts:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Tracks.</strong> At least four tracks, at most eight. You know swipe-left <strong>Add Track</strong> (and 2.0.1+ <strong>Beat</strong> / <strong>Synth</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong>). You can long-press to reorder, duplicate, or delete. You can open Instrument View from the track header.","<strong>Scenes.</strong> At least two scenes, at most eight. You can show scene numbers, launch a row, and launch single clips. You can long-press a scene number to rearrange, duplicate, or delete.","<strong>Clips.</strong> You can <strong>Add Clip</strong>, launch on the nearest downbeat, <strong>Stop Clip</strong> (and 2.0.1+ re-trigger), and long-press to move, edit, duplicate, or delete. No clip longer than 16 bars.","<strong>Mixer.</strong> You can show the mixer and use <strong>M</strong>, <strong>S</strong>, and volume sliders on purpose. On 1.0.15+, double-tap reset, Main as system volume, and up to two Main FX if present."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Pass means you did each item on this Set, not that you reread the titles. Fail means you repeat the lesson that owns the miss, then rescore."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Set you will submit to yourself for Gate B (four-plus tracks, two-plus scenes).","Walk the checklist below. Perform each line once. Write Pass or Fail in the margin.","Fix every Fail using the cited lesson. Do not start Level 3 with an open Fail on tracks, scenes, launch/stop, or mixer.","Play a one-minute pass: launch scene 1, launch scene 2, Stop Clip on one part, unmute/unsolo, global Stop.","Recite the four Gate B pillars from memory: ≥4 tracks, ≥2 scenes, mixer intentional, launch/stop fluent."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many tracks and scenes does Gate B require, and what are the hard maxima? <strong>Answer:</strong> At least 4 tracks and 2 scenes. Maxima are 8 tracks and 8 scenes. Clips max 16 bars.","What mixer moves count as \"intentional\"? <strong>Answer:</strong> You showed the mixer and used mute, solo, and/or volume on purpose — not an untouched default strip.","What does fluent launch/stop mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> Clip launch on the nearest downbeat, <strong>Stop Clip</strong> on a playing clip, scene-number launch of a row, global Stop when you mean the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.1–4.5 (tracks, clips, scenes, mixer, Session View Actions)","Note FAQ: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (reset, Main, Main FX); 1.7.2 (Track Color); 2.0.1 (Beat/Synth/Audio; stop re-trigger)","Course syllabus Gate B: ≥4 tracks, ≥2 scenes, mixer intentional, clip launch/stop fluent"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a pass/fail list for the checklist. Do not open Level 3 until every required line is Pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Gate B checklist</strong>"},{"type":"table","headers":["Item","Pass?"],"rows":[["Own Set (not a Demo)",""],["≥4 tracks, ≤8",""],["Add / reorder / duplicate or delete a track",""],["Track header opens Instrument View",""],["≥2 scenes, ≤8",""],["Show scene numbers and launch a scene",""],["Launch a single clip from another scene",""],["Add Clip + launch on nearest downbeat",""],["Stop Clip (and 2.0.1+ re-trigger if available)",""],["Long-press clip move or Duplicate/Delete",""],["No clip over 16 bars",""],["Mixer shown; M, S, and a volume move used",""],["Version noted (Help) for 1.0.15 / 1.7.2 / 2.0.1 features",""]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l2-5-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
